    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d247">

                <pb facs="1021" n="871" sameAs="frus:frus1907p2/pg871"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 4968/–2.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Minister Gummeré to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Tangier</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1907-02-16"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">February 16, 1907</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 168.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that I have
                    received from the British minister at Tangier a letter<anchor n="a"
                        xml:id="d247-f1"/> suggesting the utility of the extension by our respective
                    consular authorities at Morocco to patents of invention the same protection
                    which is now accorded to trademarks. To this end he informs me that “protection
                    will be accorded by the British consular tribunals in Morocco to American
                    patents of invention which have been duly registered in Great Britain in
                    conformity with patents, designs, and trade-marks acts, 1883–1888,” on condition
                    that protection, under the same conditions, will be assured in Morocco by the
                    American authorities to English patents of invention, and asks me to communicate
                    to him my opinion on this subject. I have the honor to inclose herewith a
                    translation of the said letter.</p>
                <p>I replied to his excellency’s letter informing him that I would submit to my
                    Government his proposition regarding the extension to patents of invention the
                    arrangement which is in force with respect to trade-marks and would inform him
                    of the decision on the subject. I have the honor to inclose herewith a
                    translation of my said reply.<anchor n="a" xml:id="d247-f2"/></p>
                <p>May I beg that the department will instruct me as to their decision on this
                    subject.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">S. R. Gummeré.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
